//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 75 — ROUND-NUMBER SUPPORT RECLAIM (FIXED PSYCHOLOGICAL GRID, BARE).
// Continues numbering after Attack 74 (weekend liquidity-vacuum breakout, DISCARDED: H1 PF 1.401 / H2
// PF 0.756, the third straight "helps H1, breaks H2" bare-breakout result). Per Attack 74's own queue
// item 8: the next cycle proposes ONE further genuinely new mechanism, distinct from the VWAP family
// and from every rejected strategy on the board (Attacks 33-65, 67/69/70/71, 72, 73, 74).
//
// CLAIM: round nominal dollar levels ($1,000 increments -- $30,000, $61,000, $100,000, etc.) act as a
// psychological support magnet in BTC, independent of any rolling technical structure: resting limit
// buys, stop-losses on short positions, and take-profits on longs cluster at round numbers because
// humans anchor to them, not because of recent swing geometry. A bar whose low pierces the nearest
// round-thousand level below the current close but whose close reclaims back above it -- while the
// PRIOR bar was already trading above that same level (a genuine retest, not a big bar blowing through
// several levels at once) -- reflects that cluster absorbing the flush.
//
// GENUINELY DISTINCT from every mechanism already tried:
//   - Attack 37/38-40/43 (liquidity sweep reversal family, CLOSED on cost by Attack 41) used the SAME
//     wick-and-reclaim SHAPE but the level was a ROLLING 20-bar swing low -- a structural, price-
//     history-dependent reference that moves every bar. This uses a FIXED, price-history-independent
//     nominal grid; the claim is psychological/order-clustering, not "recent stop-hunt geometry," and
//     the two levels frequently disagree (a round thousand is often nowhere near the last 20-bar low).
//   - Attack 66/68 (OBV divergence breakout), 72 (autocorrelation persistence), 73 (Ichimoku), 74
//     (weekend calendar) are all unrelated constructions (indicator divergence, statistical
//     persistence, multi-line trend cloud, calendar seasonality respectively).
//   - This is the "microstructure round-number behaviour" family named as still-open in
//     STRATEGY-LEDGER.md's family registry from cycle 1 of the mandate and never yet attempted.
//
// GRID CHOICE, ARGUED FROM THE MECHANISM (LESSON 49: not from either half's dates): $1,000 is the
// standard round-number granularity traders cite for BTC (round thousands), fine enough to recur
// regularly across the full 2022-2026 price range ($15.5k-$108k+) without retuning per half, coarse
// enough not to degenerate into "every bar is near a round number."
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg:
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) — EXCLUSION via rBig on rLong = close - low, never clamped. NAMED TENSION,
//        same shape as Attack 37's: this selects the DEPTH of the wick below the round level, which can
//        be shallow by construction (a bare tap). If the floor excludes most signals, that is a real
//        finding about the pattern's headroom, not a nuisance.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) — slPx = low, the actual price extreme reached during the sweep,
//        strictly below roundBelow (the signal level), never at the signal level itself.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) — LONG ONLY (fade of a failed breakdown through round-number
//        support). The mirror — a failed break UP through round-number resistance, faded short — is a
//        separate construction, following this lab's convention for a first pass (Attacks
//        50/59/63/65/66/72/73/74 all opened one-sided), and is deferred/OUTSTANDING, not built here.
//   BINDING (E17) — sweep (low < roundBelow) alone is common; sweep AND reclaim AND contTest is the
//        binding triple. Two credits were budgeted this cycle for the H1/H2 pair, so no separate
//        per-term counter build was affordable; trade count is reported as the binding evidence.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) — sweep/reclaim read a FIXED NOMINAL PRICE LEVEL (independent of recent bars);
//        contTest reads CONTINUITY across exactly one prior bar (whether the same level was already the
//        trading zone); rBig reads PRICE DISTANCE (the wick depth vs. the R floor). Three independent
//        quantities -- level, continuity, distance -- none a proxy for either other.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8, fourth-confirmation check) — sweep/reclaim/contTest are all evaluated
//        on the SAME current bar against `roundBelow`, a value derived from THIS bar's own close, and
//        `close[1]`, a value from exactly one bar back -- no state is latched across an arming event and
//        a later trigger, so the setup/trigger mutual-exclusion failure mode does not apply here, and
//        contTest is not phrased as "already true for N bars" paired with a crossing trigger, so the
//        generalised fourth-confirmation failure mode does not apply either.
//   CASCADE (HARD LESSON 42/43) — LONG at 100% equity, single entry id "L"; expect cascadeRatio 1 on
//        both halves, confirmed below.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY. No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale.
//
// FREQUENCY ESTIMATE, REGISTERED BEFORE RUNNING (HARD LESSON 4): a $1,000 grid is far denser than a
// 20-bar rolling swing low (Attack 37: 322/196 after the R floor) because round levels recur every
// 1-5% of price across this range, and price wanders through several per week. Pre-registering a WIDE
// band, honestly: 300-900 trades per half.
//
// OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17):
//   * pre-2024 half below 1.0 -> DISCARD outright. No filter stack, no rescue, H2 not run.
//   * both halves >= 1.0 AND drawdown driven by a FEW LARGE LOSSES (large avg loser vs. a steady bleed)
//     -> a sizing problem, fixable, the better candidate (board taxonomy category 1).
//   * both halves >= 1.0 but drawdown is a STEADY BLEED on a losing or barely-positive edge -> say so
//     plainly (category 2, not worth filtering per the board's own rule).
//   * both halves >= 1.0 with a bleed on a genuinely positive edge (many small losses, real PF) ->
//     category 3, the only one worth a filter stack, same standard Attack 37 earned.
//   * trade count far outside the ~60-350 workable band -> report against Attacks 33/34/36/37 for
//     context; if it clears 757 (Attack 33's cost-kill count) treat cost as a live risk, not just PF.
//   * any half under ~30 trades -> the ratio is a DIRECTION, not a result (LESSON 12).

strategy("BTC Attack75 round-number support reclaim", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

roundStep = input.float(1000.0, "Round-number grid step, USD")
rr        = input.float(2.0,  "Reward:risk")
minRpct   = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
maxBars   = input.int(192,    "Max bars in trade -- 2 days on 15m")

roundBelow = math.floor(close / roundStep) * roundStep

sweep    = low < roundBelow
reclaim  = close > roundBelow
contTest = close[1] > roundBelow
setupOn  = sweep and reclaim and contTest

minR  = close * minRpct / 100
rLong = close - low
rBig  = rLong >= minR

goLong = setupOn and rBig

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

if flat and goLong and rLong > 0
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx   := low
    tpPx   := close + rLong * rr
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(roundBelow,      title="RoundBelow")
